```
ROLE: product_realist  VOTE: red
REAL_USE_CASE: No production team needs the proposed deprioritize-or-kill mechanism. Capacity planners might reference the waste surface (high-abandonment, late-timing agent workloads) for sizing, but the reclaim mechanism itself has no adopter: the deprioritize-only path reclaims zero slots, and kill-on-flag at achievable precision destroys 2.2–18.6% of completed turns while reclaiming only 0.4–3.4% of decode (results/C2_kill_reclaim_vs_harm.csv). The only plausible adopter—an agentic serving team with >35% abandonment and late timing—still faces harm dominating benefit at every operating point.
OPERATIONAL_SIGNIFICANCE: Microbenchmark-only artifact. The L0 is a constructed trace on a CPU-only serial simulator (results/A_wasted_decode_surface.csv, results/C_reclaim_latency.csv); it does not model real GPU batching, preemption cost, or the paged-attention kernels (vLLM/SGLang) that already make preemption cheap. Deprioritization reclaims 0 slots because slowing a doomed turn does not free its batch slots (C_reclaim_latency.csv: reclaimed_slots=0 for W=1..8; p99 +18% to +317% vs +5% budget). Kill-on-flag is the only reclaim path, but at observable AUC 0.71 precision ≈0.50–0.57, yielding 1 good turn killed per doomed turn killed. The mechanism collapses to generic preemption (Llumnix/FastServe/SLO schedulers) with no agent-specific advantage demonstrated.
ADOPTION_BARRIER: [Precision ceiling: observable predictor sits 0.05–0.09 AUC below oracle (max 0.80) and oracle itself ≤0.60 when abandonment is near-coin-flip (results/B2_predictability_frontier.csv); production requires >0.9 precision to avoid SLO harm, unachievable here. Harm > benefit: kill precision 0.50–0.57 destroys 2.2–18.6% completed turns for 0.4–3.4% token reclaim (C2_kill_reclaim_vs_harm.csv). Zero reclaim via deprioritization: bounded slowdown cannot free slots, only delays termination (C_reclaim_latency.csv). Workload conditionality: >15% waste only at p_abandon≥0.35 or ≥0.20 with late timing (A_wasted_decode_surface.csv: 2.7–11.4% at realistic 10–20% abandonment); many production agent traces are lower. Prior-art redundancy: generic LLM preemption/SLO scheduling (Llumnix, FastServe, TokenFlow, FlowPrefill, SLICE) already exists; agent-specific signal shows no lift over generic preemption in this L0.]
RATIONALE: The packet’s honest-negative L0 shows the core claim fails: deprioritize-only reclaims 0 compute and violates the +5% p99 fairness budget at W=1 (+18.3%, C_reclaim_latency.csv); kill-on-flag at realistic AUC 0.71 harms more than it reclaims at every threshold (C2_kill_reclaim_vs_harm.csv). Waste >15% is conditional on high abandonment (≥35%) and late timing (A_wasted_decode_surface.csv); at 10% abandonment waste is 2.7–7.7%. Predictability is a workload property, not a model win: observable AUC 0.71 tracks an oracle ≤0.80 with a 0.05–0.09 gap, and collapses to ~0.55–0.60 when hazard_sharpness≤3 (B2_predictability_frontier.csv). The mechanism reduces to generic preemption once reclaim is attempted; the agent-abandonment angle is the only novelty candidate and is at risk. The negative result is actionable: do not pursue deprioritization for reclaim; measure the real oracle ceiling on production agent traces before investing; count re-run cost of false-positive kills in any future design. (v001)
```
